    "lawCode": "Amd §3-204, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7742\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to appointment of\ncommissioners of election\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nThis bill is intended to preserve the bipartisan nature of all boards\nof elections, as required by the New York State Constitution, by\nmaking the political party nomination of a board of elections\ncommissioner effective within 30 days of the nomination when the\nlegislative body fails to act on the nomination and there are no\nmembers of the legislative body who are members of the party making\nthe nomination.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 of the bill amends election law § 3-204(4) to provide that\nwhen a legislative body fails to act on a political party's nomination\nof a board of elections commissioner and there are no members of the\nlegislative body who are members of the party making the nomination,\nsuch appointment will take effect upon the expiration of 30 days from\nthe filing of the certificate of nomination.\n\nSection 2 of the bill is the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nArticle II, section B of the New York Constitution requires that all\nlaws creating, regulating or affecting boards of elections ensure\nequal representation of the 2 major political parties at such board.\nThe Constitution further provides that the officers of such boards\nshall be appointed in such a manner as the Legislature directs upon\nthe nomination of representatives of the each of the two major\npolitical parties. Election Law § 3-204 provides the mechanism for\nmaking the nomination and for the subsequent appointment of the\nnominee.  Specifically, the commissioners shall be appointed by the\nlegislative body in the county or, in the city of New York, by the\ncity council.  Moreover, if the legislative body fails to act within\n30 days, the members of the legislative body who are members of the\npolitical party making the nomination, may appoint the nominee.\n\nHowever, there are counties in New York which are governed by\nlegislative bodies that contain only members of one of the major\nstatewide political parties. The statute does not address how a\nnominated commissioner is appointed in such circumstances after the\nlegislative body fails to act. This bill is intended to maintain the\nbipartisan nature of the board of elections by providing that an\nappointment will take effect within 30 days of the legislative body's\nfailure to act when there are no members of the legislative body who\nare members of the party making the nomination.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATION :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
